---

<ArticleLayout
    title={title}
    description={description}
    pubDate={pubDate}
    category={category}
    categorySlug={categorySlug}
    subcategorySlug={subcategorySlug}
    readingTime={readingTime}
    image={image}
    imageAlt={imageAlt}
    headings={headings}
>
  <p class="lead">
    My coworker bought a full standing desk last year. Spent eight hundred bucks. Three months later he realized he
    barely uses the standing feature. A converter would have saved him five hundred dollars and done the same job.
  </p>

  <p>
    Tested eight desk converters over the past few months. Here's what actually works.
  </p>

  <h2 id="why-converter">When Converters Make Sense</h2>

  <p>
    A converter beats a full desk when you already have furniture you like. Renters especially - no sense buying a
    massive desk you'll have to move. Same goes if you're just testing whether standing works for you. Why commit to
    something expensive before you know?
  </p>

  <p>
    Budget matters too. Converters run thirty to fifty percent cheaper than full standing desks. That's real money.
  </p>

  <p>
    The trade-off? Less surface area. Your keyboard and monitors sit on top of the converter, and when it's raised you
    lose the desk space underneath. Not ideal if you spread papers everywhere.
  </p>

  <h2 id="converter-vs-desk">Quick Decision Guide</h2>

  <p>
    Grab a converter if budget is tight or you can't swap out your desk. They're portable and let you test the whole
    standing thing without a big commitment.
  </p>

  <p>
    Go full standing desk if you've got dedicated space, want a huge surface, and know you'll stand often. Those memory
    presets are genuinely useful when you've nailed your perfect heights.
  </p>

  <p>
    Price difference is significant. Most converters cost one fifty to four hundred bucks, and the premium ones now push past six hundred. Quality
    standing desks start around four hundred and easily hit eight hundred.
  </p>

  <h2 id="whats-new-2026">What Changed for 2026</h2>

  <p>
    Went back through every pick in September 2026. All seven are still on sale. A few changed names, and most of them
    cost more than they did when this guide first ran.
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>Varidesk is just Vari now. The Pro Plus 36 lists at $429.</li>
    <li>Mount-It sells its electric model as the MI-7927E, at $401.99.</li>
    <li>The UPLIFT E7 starts at $619, roughly double its old price.</li>
    <li>The Ergotron WorkFit-T runs $565 to $715 depending on the dealer.</li>
    <li>VIVO's converter sits around $160 on sale, up from $140.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    The tables below use current prices. The ranking stayed the same, though the UPLIFT is a much harder sell now.
  </p>

  <h2 id="quick-comparison">Quick Comparison</h2>

  <ComparisonTable columns={quickComparisonColumns} rows={quickComparisonRows}/>

  <h2 id="detailed-reviews">The Converters Worth Buying</h2>

  <h3 id="flexispot-m2b">FlexiSpot M2B</h3>

  <p>
    This one won me over. Gas spring lifts smooth with one hand. Thirty-five inch surface handles dual monitors without
    crowding. Feels solid at every height.
  </p>

  <p>
    What I like: Keyboard tray included. Five year warranty. Doesn't wobble even cranked all the way up.
  </p>

  <p>
    Downsides: Takes up real estate when lowered. Keyboard tray runs a bit shallow.
  </p>

  <p>
    Good fit for people who want reliability without paying crazy money. Around three hundred bucks.
  </p>

  <p>
    Buying today: FlexiSpot now calls it the ClassicRiser M2B. Staples lists it at $342.59 as of September 2026, so plan on closer to three fifty than three hundred.
  </p>

  <div class="my-6">
    <AffiliateButton href="/go/flexispot-m2b" text="Check FlexiSpot M2B Price"/>
  </div>

  <h3 id="vivo-converter">VIVO Height Adjustable</h3>

  <p>
    Proof you don't need three hundred plus for a working converter. Hundred forty bucks gets you a gas spring, keyboard
    tray, and thirty-two inch surface. Build quality holds up fine.
  </p>

  <p>
    What works: Ships fast from Amazon. Lift mechanism does its job. Keyboard tray actually useful.
  </p>

  <p>
    Watch out for: Smaller workspace than pricier options. Can't load it up as heavy. Gets a little wobbly at max
    height.
  </p>

  <p>
    Makes sense if you need basic standing capability and can't justify spending more.
  </p>

  <p>
    Buying today: VIVO's own store has it at $159.99 on sale (regular $199.99) as of September 2026. Still a bargain, just not a hundred forty anymore.
  </p>

  <div class="my-6">
    <AffiliateButton href="/go/vivo-converter" text="Check VIVO Price"/>
  </div>

  <h3 id="varidesk-pro">Varidesk Pro Plus Thirty-Six</h3>

  <p>
    The original. Been around forever and still holds up. Spring mechanism feels premium - smoothest lift I've tested.
    Shows up fully assembled which is honestly a relief.
  </p>

  <p>
    Strengths: Built like a tank. Rock solid stability. Customer service actually helpful. Five year warranty.
  </p>

  <p>
    Weaknesses: Pricey for what it is. Heavy at fifty-two pounds. The X-base eats into your foot space.
  </p>

  <p>
    Pick this if premium quality matters more than saving a few bucks.
  </p>

  <p>
    Buying today: The brand is simply Vari now. The Pro Plus 36 lists at $429 on vari.com and was showing out of stock in late September 2026. An electric version costs $499.
  </p>

  <div class="my-6">
    <AffiliateButton href="/go/varidesk" text="Check Varidesk Price"/>
  </div>

  <h3 id="fezibo-converter">FEZIBO Converter</h3>

  <p>
    Mid-range features without mid-range pricing. Thirty-six inch surface beats most competitors at this price point.
    Gas spring works smoothly.
  </p>

  <p>
    What's good: Big workspace for the money. Sturdy construction. Generous height adjustment range.
  </p>

  <p>
    What's not: You'll be assembling it yourself. Keyboard tray could be more comfortable. Brand isn't as established.
  </p>

  <p>
    Works well for people wanting extra surface area without dropping three hundred plus.
  </p>

  <p>
    Buying today: FEZIBO sells several sizes on Amazon. The 37 inch dual monitor model was listed at $174.95 in September 2026. These prices move week to week, so check before you buy.
  </p>

  <div class="my-6">
    <AffiliateButton href="/go/fezibo-converter" text="Check FEZIBO Price"/>
  </div>

  <h3 id="mount-it-electric">Mount-It MI-7927</h3>

  <p>
    Only electric option I'd recommend in this roundup. Hit a button and the desk raises or lowers itself. Fifty-five
    pound capacity handles heavy monitor setups. Motor stays quiet during video calls.
  </p>

  <p>
    Upsides: Zero effort to adjust. Memory presets for your favorite heights. Holds more weight than manual converters.
  </p>

  <p>
    Downsides: Needs to be near an outlet. Heavier than spring options. Motors can eventually fail.
  </p>

  <p>
    Worth it for frequent sit-stand switchers or anyone with mobility issues.
  </p>

  <p>
    Buying today: Mount-It now sells this one as the MI-7927E. It lists at $401.99 on mount-it.com, holds up to 33 pounds, and adds a built-in USB port.
  </p>

  <div class="my-6">
    <AffiliateButton href="/go/mount-it-electric" text="Check Mount-It! Price"/>
  </div>

  <h3 id="ergotron-workfit">Ergotron WorkFit-T</h3>

  <p>
    Ergotron makes mounting equipment. They know positioning. The keyboard tray placement is better than anything else I
    tested - actually matches ergonomic guidelines. Lift mechanism is whisper quiet.
  </p>

  <p>
    Strengths: Superior positioning for wrists and arms. Smoothest lift action. Build quality matches the price. Five
    year warranty.
  </p>

  <p>
    Weaknesses: Expensive. Surface runs slightly smaller. Doesn't go as low as some options.
  </p>

  <p>
    Right choice if ergonomics matter more than anything else and budget isn't tight.
  </p>

  <p>
    Buying today: This one got pricier. ErgoDirect lists the WorkFit-T at $565, and other dealers ask $699 to $715 as of September 2026. Wait for a sale if you can.
  </p>

  <div class="my-6">
    <AffiliateButton href="/go/ergotron-workfit" text="Check Ergotron Price"/>
  </div>

  <h3 id="uplift-e7">UPLIFT E7 Converter</h3>

  <p>
    Biggest workspace available. Thirty-seven inches across. Running dual large monitors or need room to spread out?
    This handles it. Gas spring manages the larger size without struggling.
  </p>

  <p>
    What's great: Massive work surface. UPLIFT brand reputation. Stable even at standing height. Good adjustment range.
  </p>

  <p>
    What's not: Takes over your desk when sitting. Heavy to move around.
  </p>

  <p>
    Fits power users running multiple monitors who need every inch of space.
  </p>

  <p>
    Buying today: The E7 starts at $619 on upliftdesk.com in September 2026. That is about double what it cost when this guide first ran, which makes it hard to call a value pick.
  </p>

  <div class="my-6">
    <AffiliateButton href="/go/uplift-converter" text="Check UPLIFT E7 Price"/>
  </div>

  <h2 id="manual-vs-electric">Gas Spring vs Electric</h2>

  <p>
    Manual converters powered by gas springs don't need power outlets. Fewer parts means less can break. They're lighter
    and cheaper too. The catch - you physically push to raise them, and no memory buttons.
  </p>

  <p>
    Electric converters raise themselves at the touch of a button. Love those memory presets. They handle heavier loads
    too. But you're tethered to an outlet, paying more money, and adding a motor that could eventually fail.
  </p>

  <p>
    Most people who transition a few times daily do fine with gas spring. Electric makes more sense for frequent
    transitions or physical limitations.
  </p>

  <ComparisonTable columns={fullComparisonColumns} rows={fullComparisonRows}/>

  <h2 id="faq">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

  <FAQSection faqs={faqItems}/>

  <h2>The Bottom Line</h2>

  <p>
    <strong>FlexiSpot M2B</strong> hits the sweet spot for most people. Smooth operation, dual monitor support, five
    year warranty, and roughly three forty in 2026. Hard to argue with that combination.
  </p>

  <p>
    Tight budget? <strong>VIVO</strong> at around a hundred sixty gets the job done.
  </p>

  <p>
    Switch positions constantly or have mobility concerns? Spend more on the <strong>Mount-It electric</strong>. That
    push-button adjustment is worth it.
  </p>

  <p>
    Whatever you pick, check your chair height once the converter is set up. If your feet stop reaching the floor,
    a <a href="/gear/best-footrests-for-desk-workers/">footrest</a> solves it for not much money.
  </p>

  <Fragment slot="full-width">
    <RelatedArticles articles={relatedArticles}/>
  </Fragment>
</ArticleLayout>
